{#ch14-0001}
# SVIPDAGSMOL

{#ch14-0002}
## {_The Ballad of Svipdag_}

{#ch14-0003}
## Introductory Note

{#ch14-0004}
The two poems, {_Grougaldr_} ({_Groa’s Spell_}) and {_Fjolsvinnsmol_} (the {_Ballad of Fjolsvith_}), which many editors have, very wisely, united under the single title of {_Svipdagsmol_}, are found only in paper manuscripts, none of them antedating the seventeenth century. Everything points to a relatively late origin for the poems: their extensive use of “kennings” or poetical circumlocutions, their romantic spirit, quite foreign to the character of the unquestionably older poems, the absence of any reference to them in the earlier documents, the frequent errors in mythology, and, finally, the fact that the poems appear to have been preserved in unusually good condition. Whether or not a connecting link of narrative verse joining the two parts has been lost is an open question; on the whole it seems likely that the story was sufficiently well known so that the reciter of the poem (or poems) merely filled in the gap with a brief prose summary in pretty much his own words. The general relationship between dialogue and narrative in the Eddic poems is discussed in the introductory note to the {_Grimnismol_}, in connection with the use of prose links.

{#ch14-0005}
The love story of Svipdag and Mengloth is not referred to elsewhere in the {_Poetic Edda_}, nor does Snorri mention it; however, Groa, who here appears as Svipdag’s mother, is spoken of by Snorri as a wise woman, the wife of Orvandil, who helps Thor with her magic charms. On the other hand, the essence of the story, the hero’s winning of a bride ringed about by flames, is strongly suggestive of parts of the Sigurth-Brynhild traditions. Whether or not it is to be regarded as a nature or solar myth depends entirely on one’s view of the whole “solar myth” school of criticism, not so highly esteemed today as formerly; such an interpretation is certainly not necessary to explain what is, under any circumstances, a very charming romance told, in the main, with dramatic effectiveness.

{#ch14-0006}
In later years the story of Svipdag and Mengloth became popular throughout the North, and was made the subject of many Danish and Swedish as well as Norwegian ballads. These have greatly assisted in the reconstruction of the outlines of the narrative surrounding the dialogue poems here given.

{#ch14-0007}
[]{.pb n="235"}I. GROUGALDR

{#ch14-0008}
Groa’s Spell

{#ch14-0009}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0010 ref="svip.1"}
::: stanza
“Wake thee, Groa\! | wake, mother good\!
At the doors of the dead I call thee;
Thy son, bethink thee, | thou badst to seek
Thy help at the hill of death.”
:::

{#ch14-0011}
{_Groa spake_}:

{#ch14-0012 ref="svip.2"}
::: stanza
“What evil vexes | mine only son,
What baleful fate hast thou found,
That thou callest thy mother, | who lies in the mould,
And the world of the living has left?”
:::

{#ch14-0013}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0014 ref="svip.3"}
::: stanza
“The woman false | whom my father embraced
Has brought me a baleful game;
For she bade me go forth | where none may fare,
And Mengloth the maid to seek.”
:::

{#ch14-0015}
{_Groa spake_}:

{#ch14-0016 ref="svip.4"}
::: stanza
“Long is the way, | long must thou wander,
But long is love as well;
Thou mayst find, perchance, | what thou fain wouldst have,
If the fates their favor will give.”
:::

{#ch14-0017}
[]{.pb n="236"}{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0018 ref="svip.5"}
::: stanza
“Charms full good | then chant to me, mother,
And seek thy son to guard;
For death do I fear | on the way I shall fare,
And in years am I young, methinks.”
:::

{#ch14-0019}
{_Groa spake_}:

{#ch14-0020 ref="svip.6"}
::: stanza
“Then first I will chant thee | the charm oft-tried,
That Rani taught to Rind;
From the shoulder whate’er | mislikes thee shake,
For helper thyself shalt thou have.
:::

{#ch14-0021 ref="svip.7"}
::: stanza
“Then next I will chant thee, | if needs thou must travel,
And wander a purposeless way:
The bolts of Urth | shall on every side
Be thy guards on the road thou goest.
:::

{#ch14-0022 ref="svip.8"}
::: stanza
“Then third I will chant thee, | if threatening streams
The danger of death shall bring:[]{.pb n="237"}
Yet to Hel shall turn | both Horn and Ruth,
And before thee the waters shall fail.
:::

{#ch14-0023 ref="svip.9"}
::: stanza
“Then fourth I will chant thee, | if come thy foes
On the gallows-way against thee:
Into thine hands | shall their hearts be given,
And peace shall the warriors wish.
:::

{#ch14-0024 ref="svip.10"}
::: stanza
“Then fifth I will chant thee, | if fetters perchance
Shall bind thy bending limbs:
O’er thy thighs do I chant | a loosening-charm,
And the lock is burst from the limbs,
And the fetters fall from the feet.
:::

{#ch14-0025 ref="svip.11"}
::: stanza
“Then sixth I will chant thee, | if storms on the sea
Have might unknown to man:
Yet never shall wind | or wave do harm,
And calm is the course of thy boat.
:::

{#ch14-0026 ref="svip.12"}
::: stanza
“Then seventh I chant thee, | if frost shall seek
To kill thee on lofty crags:
The fatal cold | shall not grip thy flesh,
And whole thy body shall be.
:::

{#ch14-0027 ref="svip.13"}
::: stanza
[]{.pb n="238"}“Then eighth will I chant thee, | if ever by night
Thou shalt wander on murky ways:
Yet never the curse | of a Christian woman
From the dead shall do thee harm.
:::

{#ch14-0028 ref="svip.14"}
::: stanza
“Then ninth will I chant thee, | if needs thou must strive
With a warlike giant in words:
Thy heart good store | of wit shall have,
And thy mouth of words full wise.
:::

{#ch14-0029 ref="svip.15"}
::: stanza
“Now fare on the way | where danger waits,
Let evils not lessen thy love\!
I have stood at the door | of the earth-fixed stones,
The while I chanted thee charms.
:::

{#ch14-0030 ref="svip.16"}
::: stanza
“Bear hence, my son, | what thy mother hath said,
And let it live in thy breast;
Thine ever shall be | the best of fortune,
So long as my words shall last.”
:::

{#ch14-0031}
[]{.pb n="239"}II. FJOLSVINNSMOL

{#ch14-0032}
The Lay of Fjolsvith

{#ch14-0033 ref="svip.17"}
::: stanza
Before the house | he beheld one coming
To the home of the giants high.
:::

{#ch14-0034}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0035 ref="svip.17"}
::: stanza
“What giant is here, | in front of the house,
And around him fires are flaming?”
:::

{#ch14-0036}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0037 ref="svip.18"}
::: stanza
“What seekest thou here? | for what is thy search?
What, friendless one, fain wouldst thou know?
By the ways so wet | must thou wander hence,
For, weakling, no home hast thou here.”
:::

{#ch14-0038}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0039 ref="svip.19"}
::: stanza
“What giant is here, | in front of the house,
To the wayfarer welcome denying?”
:::

{#ch14-0040}
[]{.pb n="240"}{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0041 ref="svip.19"}
::: stanza
“Greeting full fair | thou never shalt find,
So hence shalt thou get thee home.
:::

{#ch14-0042 ref="svip.20"}
::: stanza
“Fjolsvith am I, | and wise am I found,
But miserly am I with meat;
Thou never shalt enter | within the house,—
Go forth like a wolf on thy way!”
:::

{#ch14-0043}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0044 ref="svip.21"}
::: stanza
“Few from the joy | of their eyes will go forth,
When the sight of their loves they seek;
Full bright are the gates | of the golden hall,
And a home shall I here enjoy.”
:::

{#ch14-0045}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0046 ref="svip.22"}
::: stanza
“Tell me now, fellow, | what father thou hast,
And the kindred of whom thou camst.”
:::

{#ch14-0047}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0048 ref="svip.22"}
::: stanza
“Vindkald am I, | and Varkald’s son,
And Fjolkald his father was.
:::

{#ch14-0049 ref="svip.23"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:[]{.pb n="241"}
Who is it that holds | and has for his own
The rule of the hall so rich?”
:::

{#ch14-0050}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0051 ref="svip.24"}
::: stanza
“Mengloth is she, | her mother bore her
To the son of Svafrthorin;
She is it that holds | and has for her own
The rule of the hall so rich.”
:::

{#ch14-0052}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0053 ref="svip.25"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What call they the gate? | for among the gods
Ne’er saw man so grim a sight.”
:::

{#ch14-0054}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0055 ref="svip.26"}
::: stanza
“Thrymgjol they call it; | ’twas made by the three,
The sons of Solblindi;
And fast as a fetter | the farer it holds,
Whoever shall lift the latch.”
:::

{#ch14-0056}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0057 ref="svip.27"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:[]{.pb n="242"}
What call they the house? | for no man beheld
‘Mongst the gods so grim a sight.”
:::

{#ch14-0058}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0059 ref="svip.28"}
::: stanza
“Gastropnir is it, | of old I made it
From the limbs of Leirbrimir;
I braced it so strongly | that fast it shall stand
So long as the world shall last.”
:::

{#ch14-0060}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0061 ref="svip.29"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What call they the tree | that casts abroad
Its limbs o’er every land?”
:::

{#ch14-0062}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0063 ref="svip.30"}
::: stanza
“Mimameith its name, | and no man knows
What root beneath it runs;
And few can guess | what shall fell the tree,
For fire nor iron shall fell it.”
:::

{#ch14-0064}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0065 ref="svip.31"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:[]{.pb n="243"}
What grows from the seed | of the tree so great,
That fire nor iron shall fell?”
:::

{#ch14-0066}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0067 ref="svip.32"}
::: stanza
“Women, sick | with child, shall seek
Its fruit to the flames to bear;
Then out shall come | what within was hid,
And so is it mighty with men.”
:::

{#ch14-0068}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0069 ref="svip.33"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What cock is he | on the highest bough,
That glitters all with gold?”
:::

{#ch14-0070}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0071 ref="svip.34"}
::: stanza
“Vithofnir his name, | and now he shines
Like lightning on Mimameith’s limbs;
And great is the trouble | with which he grieves
Both Surt and Sinmora.”
:::

{#ch14-0072}
[]{.pb n="244"}{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0073 ref="svip.35"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What call they the hounds, | that before the house
So fierce and angry are?”
:::

{#ch14-0074}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0075 ref="svip.36"}
::: stanza
“Gif call they one, | and Geri the other,
If now the truth thou wouldst know;
Great they are, | and their might will grow,
Till the gods to death are doomed.”
:::

{#ch14-0076}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0077 ref="svip.37"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
May no man hope | the house to enter,
While the hungry hounds are sleeping?”
:::

{#ch14-0078}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0079 ref="svip.38"}
::: stanza
“Together they sleep not, | for so was it fixed
When the guard to them was given;
One sleeps by night, | the next by day,
So no man may enter ever.”
:::

{#ch14-0080}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0081 ref="svip.39"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:[]{.pb n="245"}
Is there no meat | that men may give them,
And leap within while they eat?”
:::

{#ch14-0082}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0083 ref="svip.40"}
::: stanza
“Two wing-joints there be | in Vithofnir’s body,
If now the truth thou wouldst know;
That alone is the meat | that men may give them,
And leap within while they eat.”
:::

{#ch14-0084}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0085 ref="svip.41"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What weapon can send | Vithofnir to seek
The house of Hel below?”
:::

{#ch14-0086}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0087 ref="svip.42"}
::: stanza
“Lævatein is there, | that Lopt with runes
Once made by the doors of death;
In Lægjarn’s chest | by Sinmora lies it,
And nine locks fasten it firm.”
:::

{#ch14-0088}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0089 ref="svip.43"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
May a man come thence | who thither goes,
And tries the sword to take?”
:::

{#ch14-0090}
[]{.pb n="246"}{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0091 ref="svip.44"}
::: stanza
“Thence may he come | who thither goes,
And tries the sword to take,
If with him he carries | what few can win,
To give to the goddess of gold.”
:::

{#ch14-0092}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0093 ref="svip.45"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What treasure is there | that men may take
To rejoice the giantess pale?”
:::

{#ch14-0094}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0095 ref="svip.46"}
::: stanza
“The sickle bright | in thy wallet bear,
Mid Vithofnir’s feathers found;
To Sinmora give it, | and then shall she grant
That the weapon by thee be won.”
:::

{#ch14-0096}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0097 ref="svip.47"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What call they the hall, | encompassed here
With flickering magic flames?”
:::

{#ch14-0098}
[]{.pb n="247"}{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0099 ref="svip.48"}
::: stanza
“Lyr is it called, | and long it shall
On the tip of a spear-point tremble;
Of the noble house | mankind has heard,
But more has it never known.”
:::

{#ch14-0100}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0101 ref="svip.49"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What one of the gods | has made so great
The hall I behold within?”
:::

{#ch14-0102}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0103 ref="svip.50"}
::: stanza
“Uni and Iri, | Bari and Jari,
Var and Vegdrasil,
Dori and Ori, | Delling, and there
Was Loki, the fear of the folk.”
:::

{#ch14-0104}
[]{.pb n="248"}{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0105 ref="svip.51"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What call they the mountain | on which the maid
Is lying so lovely to see?”
:::

{#ch14-0106}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0107 ref="svip.52"}
::: stanza
“Lyfjaberg is it, | and long shall it be
A joy to the sick and the sore;
For well shall grow | each woman who climbs it,
Though sick full long she has lain.”
:::

{#ch14-0108}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0109 ref="svip.53"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
What maidens are they | that at Mengloth’s knees
Are sitting so gladly together?”
:::

{#ch14-0110}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0111 ref="svip.54"}
::: stanza
“Hlif is one named, | Hlifthrasa another,
Thjothvara call they the third;[]{.pb n="249"}
Bjort and Bleik, | Blith and Frith,
Eir and Aurbotha.”
:::

{#ch14-0112}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0113 ref="svip.55"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
Aid bring they to all | who offerings give,
If need be found therefor?”
:::

{#ch14-0114}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0115 ref="svip.56"}
::: stanza
“Soon aid they all | who offerings give
On the holy altars high;
And if danger they see | for the sons of men,
Then each from ill do they guard.”
:::

{#ch14-0116}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0117 ref="svip.57"}
::: stanza
“Now answer me, Fjolsvith, | the question I ask,
For now the truth would I know:
Lives there the man | who in Mengloth’s arms
So fair may seek to sleep?”
:::

{#ch14-0118}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0119 ref="svip.58"}
::: stanza
“No man there is | who in Mengloth’s arms
So fair may seek to sleep,
Save Svipdag alone, | for the sun-bright maid
Is destined his bride to be.”
:::

{#ch14-0120}
[]{.pb n="250"}{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0121 ref="svip.59"}
::: stanza
“Fling back the gates\! | make the gateway wide\!
Here mayst thou Svipdag see\!
Hence get thee to find | if gladness soon
Mengloth to me will give.”
:::

{#ch14-0122}
{_Fjolsvith spake_}:

{#ch14-0123 ref="svip.60"}
::: stanza
“Hearken, Mengloth, | a man is come;
Go thou the guest to see\!
The hounds are fawning, | the house bursts open,—
Svipdag, methinks, is there.”
:::

{#ch14-0124}
{_Mengloth spake_}:

{#ch14-0125 ref="svip.61"}
::: stanza
“On the gallows high | shall hungry ravens
Soon thine eyes pluck out,
If thou liest in saying | that here at last
The hero is come to my hall.
:::

{#ch14-0126 ref="svip.62"}
::: stanza
“Whence camest thou hither? | how camest thou here?
What name do thy kinsmen call thee?
Thy race and thy name | as a sign must I know,
That thy bride I am destined to be.”
:::

{#ch14-0127}
{_Svipdag spake_}:

{#ch14-0128 ref="svip.63"}
::: stanza
“Svipdag am I, | and Solbjart’s son;
Thence came I by wind-cold ways;[]{.pb n="251"}
With the words of Urth | shall no man war,
Though unearned her gifts be given.”
:::

{#ch14-0129}
{_Mengloth spake_}:

{#ch14-0130 ref="svip.64"}
::: stanza
“Welcome thou art, | for long have I waited;
The welcoming kiss shalt thou win\!
For two who love | is the longed-for meeting
The greatest gladness of all.
:::

{#ch14-0131 ref="svip.65"}
::: stanza
“Long have I sat | on Lyfjaberg here,
Awaiting thee day by day;
And now I have | what I ever hoped,
For here thou art come to my hall.
:::

{#ch14-0132 ref="svip.66"}
::: stanza
“Alike we yearned; | I longed for thee,
And thou for my love hast longed;
But now henceforth | together we know
Our lives to the end we shall live.”
:::

{#ch14-0133 on="svip.1"}
::::: commentary
[]{.pb n="234"}[]{.pb n="235"}{_Svipdag_} (“Swift Day”): the names of the speakers are lacking in the manuscripts.
:::::

{#ch14-0134 on="svip.3"}
::::: commentary
{_The woman_}: Svipdag’s stepmother, who is responsible for []{.pb n="236"}his search for {_Mengloth_} (“Necklace-Glad”). This name has suggested that Mengloth is really {_Frigg_}, possessor of the famous Brisings’ necklace, or else Freyja (cf. {_Lokasenna_}, 20, note).
:::::

{#ch14-0135 on="svip.6"}
::::: commentary
For this catalogue of charms (stanzas 6–14) cf. the {_Ljothatal_} ({_Hovamol_}, 147–165). {_Rani_} and {_Rind_}: the manuscripts have these words in inverse relation; I have followed Neckel’s emendation. Rind was the giantess who became the mother of Vali, Othin’s son, the one-night-old avenger of Baldr (cf. {_Voluspo_}, 33–34, and {_Baldrs Draumar_}, 11 and note). Rani is presumably Othin, who, according to a skaldic poem, won Rind by magic.
:::::

{#ch14-0136 on="svip.7"}
::::: commentary
{_Urth_}: one of the three Norns, or Fates; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 20.[]{.pb n="237"}
:::::

{#ch14-0137 on="svip.8"}
::::: commentary
{_Horn_} and {_Ruth_}: these two rivers, here used merely to symbolize all dangerous streams, are not included in the catalogue of rivers given in {_Grimnismol_}, 27–29, for which reason some editors have changed the names to Hron and Hrith.
:::::

{#ch14-0138 on="svip.10"}
::::: commentary
This stanza is a close parallel to {_Hovamol_}, 150, and the fifth line may well be an interpolation from line 4 of that stanza.[]{.pb n="238"}
:::::

{#ch14-0139 on="svip.13"}
::::: commentary
{_A dead Christian woman_}: this passage has distressed many editors, who have sought to emend the text so as to make it mean simply “a dead witch.” The fact seems to be, however, that this particular charm was composed at a time when Christians were regarded by all conservative pagans as emissaries of darkness. A dead woman’s curse would naturally be more potent, whether she was Christian or otherwise, than a living one’s. Presumably this charm is much older than the poem in which it here stands.
:::::

{#ch14-0140 on="svip.16"}
::::: commentary
At this point Groa’s song ends, and Svipdag, thus fortified, goes to seek Mengloth. All the link that is needed between the poems is approximately this: “Then Svipdag searched long for []{.pb n="239"}Mengloth, and at last he came to a great house set all about with flames. And before the house there was a giant.”
:::::

{#ch14-0141 on="svip.17"}
::::: commentary
Most editors have here begun a new series of stanza numbers, but if the {_Grougaldr_} and the {_Fjolsvinnsmol_} are to be considered as a single poem, it seems more reasonable to continue the stanza numbers consecutively. Bugge thinks a stanza has been lost before 17, including Fjolsvith’s name, so that the “he” in line 1 might have something to refer to. However, just such a prose link as I have suggested in the note on stanza 16 would serve the purpose. Editors have suggested various rearrangements in the lines of stanzas 17–19. The substance, however, is clear enough. The giant {_Fjolsvith_} (“Much-Wise”), the warder of the house in which Mengloth dwells, sees Svipdag coming and stops him with the customary threats. The assignment of the []{.pb n="240"}speeches in stanzas 17–20, in the absence of any indications in the manuscripts, is more or less guesswork.
:::::

{#ch14-0142 on="svip.22"}
::::: commentary
{_Vindkald_} (“Wind-Cold”), {_Varkald_} (“Cold of Early Spring”) and {_Fjolkald_} (“Much Cold”): Svipdag apparently seeks to persuade Fjolsvith that he belongs to the frost giants.[]{.pb n="241"}
:::::

{#ch14-0143 on="svip.24"}
::::: commentary
{_Svafrthorin_}: who he was, or what his name means, or who his son was, are all unknown.
:::::

{#ch14-0144 on="svip.26"}
::::: commentary
{_Thrymgjol_} (“Loud-Clanging”): this gate, like the gate of the dead, shuts so fast as to trap those who attempt to use it (cf. {_Sigurtharkvitha en skamma_}, 68 and note). It was made by the dwarfs, sons of {_Solblindi_} (“Sun-Blinded”), the traditional craftsmen, who could not endure the light of day.[]{.pb n="242"}
:::::

{#ch14-0145 on="svip.28"}
::::: commentary
{_Gastropnir_}: “Guest-Crusher.” {_Leirbrimir’s_} (“Clay-Giant’s”) {_limbs_}: a poetic circumlocution for “clay”; cf. the description of the making of earth from the body of the giant Ymir, {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 21.
:::::

{#ch14-0146 on="svip.30"}
::::: commentary
{_Mimameith_} (“Mimir’s Tree”): the ash Yggdrasil, that overshadows the whole world. The well of Mimir was situated at its base; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 27–29.[]{.pb n="243"}
:::::

{#ch14-0147 on="svip.32"}
::::: commentary
Gering suggests that two stanzas have been lost between stanzas 15 and 16, but the giant’s answer fits the question quite well enough. The fruit of Yggdrasil, when cooked, is here assumed to have the power of assuring safe childbirth.
:::::

{#ch14-0148 on="svip.34"}
::::: commentary
{_Vithofnir_} (“Tree-Snake”): apparently identical with either the cock Gollinkambi (cf. {_Voluspo_}, 43) or Fjalar (cf. {_Voluspo_}, 42), the former of which wakes the gods to battle, and the latter the giants. {_Surt_}: the giant mentioned in {_Voluspo_}, 52, as ruler of the fire-world; here used to represent the giants in general, who are constantly in terror of the cock’s eternal watchfulness. {_Sinmora_}: presumably Surt’s wife, the giantess who possesses the weapon by which alone the cock Vithofnir may be slain.[]{.pb n="244"}
:::::

{#ch14-0149 on="svip.35"}
::::: commentary
The last two lines have been variously emended.
:::::

{#ch14-0150 on="svip.36"}
::::: commentary
{_Gif_} and {_Geri_}: both names signify “Greedy.” The first part of line 3 is conjectural; the manuscripts indicate the word “eleven,” which clearly fails to make sense.[]{.pb n="245"}
:::::

{#ch14-0151 on="svip.42"}
::::: commentary
{_Lævatein_} (“Wounding Wand”): the manuscripts differ as to the form of this name. The suggestion that the reference is to the mistletoe with which Baldr was killed seems hardly reasonable. {_Lopt_}: Loki. {_Lægjarn_} (“Lover of Ill”): Loki; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 35, []{.pb n="246"}where the term appears as an adjective applied to Loki. This is Falk’s emendation for the manuscripts’ “Sægjarn,” meaning “Sea Lover.” {_Sinmora_}: cf. stanza 34.
:::::

{#ch14-0152 on="svip.44"}
::::: commentary
{_Goddess of gold_}: poetic circumlocution for “woman,” here meaning Sinmora.
:::::

{#ch14-0153 on="svip.46"}
::::: commentary
{_Sickle_}: i.e., tail feather. With this the circle of impossibilities is completed. To get past the dogs, they must be fed with the wing-joints of the cock Vithofnir; the cock can be killed only []{.pb n="247"}with the sword in Sinmora’s possession, and Sinmora will give up the sword only in return for the tail feather of the cock.
:::::

{#ch14-0154 on="svip.48"}
::::: commentary
{_Lyr_} (“Heat-Holding”): just what the spear-point reference means is not altogether clear. Presumably it refers to the way in which the glowing brightness of the lofty hall makes it seem to quiver and turn in the air, but the tradition, never baffled by physical laws, may have actually balanced the whole building on a single point to add to the difficulties of entrance.
:::::

{#ch14-0155 on="svip.50"}
::::: commentary
{_Loki_}, the one god named, was the builder of the hall, with the aid of the nine dwarfs. {_Jari_}, {_Dori_}, and {_Ori_} appear in the {_Voluspo_} catalogue of the dwarfs (stanzas 13 and 15); {_Delling_} appears in {_Hovamol_}, 161, and {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 25, in the latter case, however, the name quite possibly referring to some one else. The other dwarfs’ names do not appear elsewhere. The manuscripts differ as to the forms of many of these names.[]{.pb n="248"}
:::::

{#ch14-0156 on="svip.52"}
::::: commentary
{_Lyfjaberg_} (“Hill of Healing”): the manuscripts vary as to this name; I have followed Bugge’s suggestion. This stanza implies that Mengloth is a goddess of healing, and hence, perhaps, an hypostasis of Frigg, as already intimated by her name (cf. stanza 3, note). In stanza 54 Eir appears as one of Mengloth’s handmaidens, and Eir, according to Snorri ({_Gylfaginning_}, 35) is herself the Norse Hygeia. Compare this stanza with stanza 32.
:::::

{#ch14-0157 on="svip.54"}
::::: commentary
The manuscripts and editions show many variations in these names. They may be approximately rendered thus: Helper, Help-Breather, Folk-Guardian, Shining, White, Blithe, Peaceful, Kindly (?), and Gold-Giver.[]{.pb n="249"}
:::::

{#ch14-0158 on="svip.55"}
::::: commentary
One of the manuscripts omits stanzas 55 and 56.
:::::

{#ch14-0159 on="svip.56"}
::::: commentary
The first line is based on a conjectural emendation.[]{.pb n="250"}
:::::

{#ch14-0160 on="svip.63"}
::::: commentary
{_Solbjart_} (“Sun-Bright”): not elsewhere mentioned. {_The words of Urth_}: i.e., the decrees of fate; cf. stanza 7.[]{.pb n="251"}
:::::

{#ch14-0161 on="svip.65"}
::::: commentary
{_Lyfjaberg_} cf. stanza 52 and note.
:::::

